{"citation": "Runway 01L/19R geometry (9,400 ft × 150 ft) and ALSF-2 approach lighting supporting Cat II/III precision approaches, per FlightAware airport data and FAA ALSF-2 system specifications.", "claim": "At 6 a.m. a United widebody breaks out of the clouds on the Runway 1L approach at Dulles, and the first thing its crew looks for is a 2,400-foot ladder of light—an ALSF-2 approach lighting system, the standard for the Category II/III precision approaches this runway is built to fly.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-01", "evidence_ids": ["quantitative-analyst::E-0034", "quantitative-analyst::E-0041"], "footnote_id": "1", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "FlightAware record (E-0034) confirms 9,400 ft × 150 ft for Runway 01L/19R. E-0041 confirms ALSF-2 standard extent of 2,400 ft from FAA ALSF-2 system specifications. Cat II/III support on IAD 01L is consistent with published ILS procedures.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "FAA \"Thermal Exhaust Plume Impact on Airport Operations: Technical Guidance and Assessment Tool\" (plume magnitude driven by on-site generation; overall disruption risk rated low, significant mainly to light aircraft in calm/cold/unstable conditions); FAA Procedures for Handling Airspace Matters, Ch. 6 (EMI to navigation/radar treated as a substantial adverse effect).", "claim": "Plume magnitude is largely a function of on-site power generation; glare is subject to a published FAA screen; EMI to a NAVAID or radar is a recognized \"substantial adverse effect\" that gets studied, not assumed.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-02", "draft_sha256": "91d48cc40452b41e7679e82f8274c19686f54bd309a73abc3be87049656cb552", "evidence_ids": ["regulatory-political-analyst::E-0088", "operations-analyst::E-0031"], "footnote_id": "2", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0088 quotes FAA Technical Guidance: overall plume risk rated low, significant to light aircraft in critical phases. E-0031 quotes FAA PHAM Ch. 6 Sec. 3 verbatim on EMI as substantial adverse effect. Both records marked usable.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "\"Dulles Master Plan Update Sets Framework for Decades of Airport Development\" (MWAA, July 16, 2025); \"New Master Plan for Dulles Airport Envisions Growth to 90 Million Annual Passengers\" (fifth runway planned since the 1950s, in the 1985 master plan, FAA-approved with the fourth runway; 88-gate expansion from 130 to 218 gates by 2080–2090).", "claim": "On July 16, 2025, the MWAA Board reaffirmed a master plan envisioning growth toward roughly 90 million annual passengers, a fifth runway, and 88 new gates—a build-out planned in some form since the 1950s, carried in the 1985 master plan, and FAA-approved alongside the fourth runway.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-03", "draft_sha256": "91d48cc40452b41e7679e82f8274c19686f54bd309a73abc3be87049656cb552", "evidence_ids": ["airline-commercial-strategist::E-0014", "chief-engineer::E-0079"], "footnote_id": "3", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "Board date July 16, 2025 and 90M passenger target confirmed by live fetches of MWAA press release and ffxnow.com article. Corrected '24 new gates' to '88 new gates': the ffxnow.com source (E-0079's own cited article) states gate expansion from 130 to 218 (88 more); evidence record E-0079 had extracted '24 gates,' which does not appear in the source. Fifth runway confirmed. 1950s/1985 plan history supported by E-0014.", "verification_status": "corrected"}
{"citation": "\"Dulles Airport's 2024 Passenger Total Set New All-Time Record\" (27.25 million passengers, 2024 all-time record); \"Washington Dulles' 5 Largest Airlines by Market Share\" (United ≈70% of flights; next carrier ≈5%).", "claim": "Dulles set an all-time record of 27.25 million passengers in 2024, and United flies roughly 70% of its departures—a concentrated, growing hub with a single carrier whose network depends on this airfield.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-04", "draft_sha256": "91d48cc40452b41e7679e82f8274c19686f54bd309a73abc3be87049656cb552", "evidence_ids": ["airline-commercial-strategist::E-0010", "airline-commercial-strategist::E-0009"], "footnote_id": "4", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "27.25 million passengers confirmed by live fetch of ffxnow.com. United percentage corrected: live fetch of Simple Flying source returned 70.42% for United and 5.12% for Delta; evidence record E-0009 had cited 68%/4%, which does not match the actual article. Draft updated to 'roughly 70%' and footnote updated to '≈70%/≈5%'. Correction strengthens the single-carrier concentration argument.", "verification_status": "corrected"}
{"citation": "14 CFR 77.19, Civil Airport Imaginary Surfaces (precision approach surface, 50:1 for the first 10,000 ft); analyst construction from the 50:1 slope: a 50-ft object reaches the surface ~2,500 ft from origin.", "claim": "Under 14 CFR 77.19, a precision runway's approach surface rises at just 50:1, so a 50-foot object reaches the surface only about 2,500 feet from its origin—and data-hall roofs with rooftop chillers and exhaust stacks routinely exceed 50 feet.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-05", "draft_sha256": "91d48cc40452b41e7679e82f8274c19686f54bd309a73abc3be87049656cb552", "evidence_ids": ["quantitative-analyst::E-0035", "quantitative-analyst::E-0036"], "footnote_id": "5", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0035 quotes 14 CFR 77.19 eCFR text verbatim: '50 to 1' slope for precision instrument runways. E-0036 is correct arithmetic (50 ft ÷ 50 = 2,500 ft from origin). eCFR URL redirected during live fetch due to anti-bot protection; regulatory text is established and consistent with verbatim record quote.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "FAA AC 150/5300-13B, Airport Design (departure/takeoff clearance surface, 40:1): 25 ft of allowable object height at 1,000 ft off the departure end.", "claim": "The departure surface is tighter still, allowing only 25 feet of height 1,000 feet off the departure end.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-06", "draft_sha256": "91d48cc40452b41e7679e82f8274c19686f54bd309a73abc3be87049656cb552", "evidence_ids": ["quantitative-analyst::E-0037"], "footnote_id": "6", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0037 cites AC 150/5300-13B and TERPS 40:1 OCS; arithmetic 1,000 ÷ 40 = 25 ft is correct. Evidence record is contextual; AC 150/5300-13B was not directly fetched. Claim is consistent with published TERPS departure obstacle clearance surface standards.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "Analyst construction from standard 3-degree glidepath geometry (50 ft threshold crossing height): ≈102 ft AGL at 1,000 ft, ≈155 ft at 2,000 ft, ≈207 ft at 3,000 ft from the threshold.", "claim": "And whatever is built there—plume, glare, or structure—sits in the band where an aircraft on a standard 3-degree glidepath is barely 200 feet above the ground at 3,000 feet from the threshold.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-07", "draft_sha256": "91d48cc40452b41e7679e82f8274c19686f54bd309a73abc3be87049656cb552", "evidence_ids": ["quantitative-analyst::E-0040"], "footnote_id": "7", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0040 calculates ≈207 ft AGL at 3,000 ft from threshold using standard 3-degree glidepath with 50 ft TCH. The phrase 'barely 200 feet' accurately characterizes 207 ft as just above 200 ft.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "14 CFR Part 77 (§77.9 notice via Form 7460-1; obstruction evaluation ending in a Determination of No Hazard or Hazard; $1,000/day civil penalty for knowing/willful failure to notify).", "claim": "Construction in an approach surface requires a Form 7460-1 filing and an FAA obstruction evaluation ending in a Determination of No Hazard or Hazard; skipping the notice carries a $1,000-per-day civil penalty.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-08", "draft_sha256": "91d48cc40452b41e7679e82f8274c19686f54bd309a73abc3be87049656cb552", "evidence_ids": ["airport-coo::E-0058", "chief-engineer::E-0072", "chief-engineer::E-0073", "regulatory-political-analyst::E-0082"], "footnote_id": "8", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0073 quotes FAA Part 77 page: '$1,000 per day until the notice is received' for knowing/willful violation. E-0058, E-0072, E-0082 corroborate the 7460-1 process and OE/AAA outcome. FAA Part 77 page returned HTTP 403 during live fetch; E-0073 is marked usable and corroborated by three additional records.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "FAA Airport Sponsor Assurances (Grant Assurances 19, 20, 21, 22, 29); 49 U.S.C. 47107(a)(9) (statutory basis for GA 20, protect terminal airspace) and 47107(a)(10) (statutory basis for GA 21, compatible land use).", "claim": "Grant Assurances 19, 20, 21, 22, and 29 bind MWAA, and 20 and 21 trace to statute—49 U.S.C. 47107(a)(9) and (10)—obligating the sponsor to protect terminal airspace and keep nearby land compatible.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-09", "evidence_ids": ["airport-coo::E-0059", "regulatory-political-analyst::E-0085", "regulatory-political-analyst::E-0086", "regulatory-political-analyst::E-0087"], "footnote_id": "9", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0086 quotes 49 U.S.C. 47107(a)(9) verbatim; E-0087 quotes 47107(a)(10) verbatim. E-0085 and E-0059 confirm mapping of GA 20 to 47107(a)(9) and GA 21 to 47107(a)(10). All four records marked usable.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "Section 163, FAA Reauthorization Act of 2018, limiting FAA authority over on-airport land use to development affecting (A) safe/efficient aircraft operation, (B) safety of persons/property on the ground, or (C) the value of prior federal investment.", "claim": "Frame the objection on the exact standard the FAA still applies after Section 163 of the 2018 Reauthorization Act narrowed its reach: whether development (A) materially impacts safe and efficient aircraft operation, (B) endangers people or property on the ground, or (C) diminishes the value of prior federal investment.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-10", "draft_sha256": "91d48cc40452b41e7679e82f8274c19686f54bd309a73abc3be87049656cb552", "evidence_ids": ["regulatory-political-analyst::E-0083"], "footnote_id": "10", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0083 quotes the three prongs from Kaplan Kirsch legal analysis of Section 163. Note: E-0083 records that Section 743 of the 2024 reauthorization revised Section 163; effect on the (A)/(B)/(C) framework is not documented in the evidence ledger. No change made but flagged for counsel review.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "\"Dulles Airport Lands $41M Federal Grant for Concourse E Construction\"; FAA Airport Infrastructure Grant, FY2026 (~$41.8M + ~$22.1M ≈ $64M)—a documented floor for the prior-federal-investment interest Grant Assurance 29 and Section 163(C) protect.", "claim": "That last prong is not abstract; two 2026 federal grants alone put roughly $64 million into Dulles—a documented floor beneath the decades of AIP-funded airfield and NAVAID value at stake.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-11", "draft_sha256": "91d48cc40452b41e7679e82f8274c19686f54bd309a73abc3be87049656cb552", "evidence_ids": ["quantitative-analyst::E-0043"], "footnote_id": "11", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "Grant arithmetic verified: E-0043 cites $41.8M (Concourse E) + $22,121,621 (AIG FY2026) = ~$63.9M ≈ $64M. Corrected footnote: removed '(C)' from 'Grant Assurance 29' — GA 29 concerns Airport Layout Plan compliance and has no '(C)' subpart; the prior-federal-investment prong belongs solely to Section 163(C).", "verification_status": "corrected"}
{"citation": "FAA AC 150/5200-33C, Hazardous Wildlife Attractants On or Near Airports (current; supersedes 33B, which is cancelled), recommending 10,000 ft separation for turbine-aircraft airports and up to 5 statute miles where an attractant could move wildlife into approach/departure airspace; analyst construction placing a runway-end pond ~95% inside the 10,000-ft standard.", "claim": "The stormwater pond compounds the problem: current guidance in AC 150/5200-33C recommends 10,000 feet of separation from turbine-aircraft movement areas, and a pond at the runway end sits almost entirely inside that standard.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-12", "draft_sha256": "91d48cc40452b41e7679e82f8274c19686f54bd309a73abc3be87049656cb552", "evidence_ids": ["operations-analyst::E-0028", "quantitative-analyst::E-0038", "quantitative-analyst::E-0039", "operations-analyst::E-0029"], "footnote_id": "12", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0028 and E-0038 confirm AC 150/5200-33C 10,000-ft standard for turbine-aircraft airports. E-0029 confirms 33B is cancelled and 33C is current. The '~95% inside' figure (E-0039) is analyst arithmetic using an assumed actual distance of 500 ft, not a surveyed measurement. Claim retained because any runway-end pond is dramatically inside the 10,000-ft standard regardless of the exact measured distance.", "verification_status": "qualified"}
{"citation": "\"The Airports Authority Announces Sale of 424 Acres of Western Lands at Dulles International\" (MWAA press release, 2018): $236.5 million; ~$558,000/acre; Digital Realty; \"Airport Land Sale Expected to Help Lower Dulles Ticket Prices\" (~$207 million earmarked to reduce airline rentals, fees, and charges).", "claim": "In 2018 it sold 424 acres of Dulles \"Western Lands\" to Digital Realty for $236.5 million—about $558,000 an acre—netting roughly $207 million earmarked to lower airline costs.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-13", "draft_sha256": "91d48cc40452b41e7679e82f8274c19686f54bd309a73abc3be87049656cb552", "evidence_ids": ["infrastructure-economist::E-0001", "contrarian::E-0018", "regulatory-political-analyst::E-0090", "contrarian::E-0019"], "footnote_id": "13", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "Live fetch of MWAA press release confirmed: 424 acres, $236.5 million, Digital Realty, September 2018, earmarked for CPE reduction. Per-acre calculation ($236.5M ÷ 424 = $557,783 ≈ $558,000) is correct. The ~$207M net is supported by E-0018. E-0068 (LandApp blog, '433 acres') is a disputed secondary source contradicted by the primary MWAA document; 424 acres is correct.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "\"Data Center Alley: Ashburn, VA\" (Ashburn/Loudoun estimated to carry ~70% of global internet traffic); ACI, \"Airport Non-Aeronautical Revenues\" (~46% of total U.S. airport revenue is non-aeronautical, per 2025 ACI-NA Concessions Benchmarking Survey).", "claim": "Ashburn next door carries an estimated 70% of global internet traffic; airport-adjacent, power-and-fiber-served land is among the most valuable non-aeronautical acreage in American aviation, and non-aeronautical revenue is already about 46% of U.S. airport income.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-14", "evidence_ids": ["contrarian::E-0024", "aviation-historian::E-0066"], "footnote_id": "14", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0024 quotes Lightyear.ai guide: 'an estimated 70% of global internet traffic passes through it.' E-0066 cites 2025 ACI-NA Concessions Benchmarking Survey for the 46% figure. Both records contextual; the 70% is a widely cited industry estimate and the draft correctly uses 'estimated.' The 46% is attributed to a named industry survey.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "FAA Airport Improvement Program Grant Assurances (GA 24, self-sustaining rate structure); FAA \"Thermal Exhaust Plume Impact on Airport Operations: Technical Guidance and Assessment Tool\" (overall flight-disruption risk rated low; significant mainly to light aircraft in calm/cold/unstable conditions).", "claim": "the FAA rates the overall plume flight-disruption risk as low, significant mainly to light aircraft in calm, cold, or unstable conditions—not to the transport-category jets that serve this airport.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-15", "draft_sha256": "91d48cc40452b41e7679e82f8274c19686f54bd309a73abc3be87049656cb552", "evidence_ids": ["regulatory-political-analyst::E-0088", "contrarian::E-0020"], "footnote_id": "15", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0088 quotes FAA Technical Guidance: 'overall risk...low...significant turbulent effects...most critical during low altitude flight in calm and cold air.' E-0020 confirms GA 24. The inference that transport-category jets face lower plume risk than light aircraft is logically sound from the source; the final draft makes this inference explicit rather than leaving 'for the transport-category jets' as an unsupported qualifier. Qualified to reflect this is an inference from the stated principle.", "verification_status": "qualified"}
{"citation": "\"The Airports Authority Announces Sale of 424 Acres of Western Lands at Dulles International\" (MWAA press release): Western Lands were part of an 854-acre tract acquired 2005–2007; surplus remaining after the fourth runway and support area were built.", "claim": "The Western Lands were the surplus that remained after the fourth runway and its support area were built.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-16", "draft_sha256": "91d48cc40452b41e7679e82f8274c19686f54bd309a73abc3be87049656cb552", "evidence_ids": ["contrarian::E-0019", "regulatory-political-analyst::E-0090"], "footnote_id": "16", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0019 quotes the MWAA press release verbatim: 'After building the runway and support area, the Western Lands portion of the property has remained undeveloped.' E-0090 corroborates from the same primary source. Live fetch of the MWAA press release confirmed these facts.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "MWAA History and Facts (federal lease under the Metropolitan Washington Airports Act of 1986, executed June 7, 1987, extended to 2100); \"DOT Secretary Sean Duffy Launches New Initiative to Revitalize Dulles Airport.\"", "claim": "The Authority operates Dulles under a federal lease running to 2100, under active federal pressure to modernize the airport.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-17", "draft_sha256": "91d48cc40452b41e7679e82f8274c19686f54bd309a73abc3be87049656cb552", "evidence_ids": ["regulatory-political-analyst::E-0092", "regulatory-political-analyst::E-0093"], "footnote_id": "17", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0092 quotes MWAA History and Facts page: 'lease of the Airports will expire in 2100'; original 1987 lease, extended in 2024 by 33 years. E-0093 cites FAA newsroom for the Duffy/DOT modernization initiative. Both records marked usable.", "verification_status": "verified"}
